.claude-plugin/plugin.json
.claude-plugin/marketplace.json

# A CSS file whose Hugo template code lives inside a CSS comment, so Hugo can
# derive the variables at build time. Prettier's CSS parser crashes on the
# template actions, and renaming it so the go-template parser picks it up makes
# things worse: that parser reflows the comment and drops the :root block. There
# is no extension or edit that makes this file safe to format.
assets/css/variables.css

# A Hugo template that carries the search palette's translated strings. They are
# emitted with jsonify, which supplies its own quotes and escapes what is inside
# them - Hugo applies no contextual escaping to a .js template, so a translation
# carrying an apostrophe or a double quote would otherwise terminate a
# hand-written literal and leave the file unparseable. Prettier reads the file as
# JavaScript, where a bare template action in expression position is a syntax
# error, so it cannot format it at all. Wrapping the actions back in quotes to
# satisfy the formatter is the bug.
assets/js/search-dialog.js

# A Hugo template that emits the FlexSearch index, so it opens with a template
# action and is not valid JSON. Renaming it so prettier skips it is not an
# option either: Hugo derives the media type from the extension, and a non-.json
# source leaves the generated file with no mimetype, which fails minification.
assets/json/search-data.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Hugo templates
#
# prettier-plugin-go-template cannot format Hugo templates without changing what
# they render. Three failures, each reproduced by building the site and diffing
# all 302 pages:
#
#   1. It reflows a single-line attribute across lines, and the newlines land
#      inside the attribute value. `aria-expanded="{{ if $open }}true{{ else }}
#      false{{ end }}"` renders as aria-expanded=" false " on every collapsible
#      sidebar section, breaking ARIA on 259 of 302 pages.
#   2. It moves the closing `-}}` of a multi-line `{{- /* ... */ -}}` comment onto
#      its own line, splitting `*/ -}}` into something Hugo cannot parse. ~94
#      templates carry such comments.
#   3. It drops significant spaces next to template actions, merging CSS class
#      names and deleting separators (see the title separator in head.html).
#
# Formatting these by hand where it mattered is what the fixes above do; running
# the formatter over all of them is not safe.

# ----------------------------------------------------------- Generated / vendored
#
# Build output and third-party bundles: regenerated or copied verbatim, so
# formatting them is churn at best and expands minified bundles at worst.
assets/css/compiled/
assets/js/vendor/
docs/hugo_stats.json
docs/public/

# ------------------------------------------------- templates that cannot be formatted
#
# Each of these opens (or interleaves) a multi-line doc comment whose surrounding
# whitespace is trimmed by the comment's own `-}}`, and whose neighbour is literal
# HTML rather than another action. Prettier splits `*/ -}}` on such a comment, and
# every way of avoiding that leaks whitespace into the output instead:
#   * the no-trim `{{/* ... */}}` form emits the whitespace the comment used to trim;
#   * an ignore fence must also use the no-trim form (the plugin does not recognise
#     `{{- /* prettier-ignore-start */ -}}`), so the fence leaks in its place.
# Nothing adjacent can absorb it, so these keep their doc comments and exact output.
# Every other template under layouts/ is formatted.
layouts/_partials/blog/identity.html
layouts/_partials/blog/rail.html
layouts/_partials/blog/widget-heading.html
layouts/_partials/components/codeblock.html
layouts/_partials/scripts/typeit.html
layouts/_partials/scripts/youtube-lite.html

# ------------------------------------ templates with spaces next to an action
#
# These carry a significant space immediately next to a template action inside a
# tag, either separating attributes or separating class names:
#
#   {{ if $enableImageZoom }} data-zoomable{{ end }}{{ if $lazyLoading }} loading="lazy"{{ end }}
#   class="hextra-list{{ if $cardView }} hextra-list--cards{{ end }} not-prose"
#
# Prettier deletes that space, fusing `data-zoomableloading=lazy` or two class
# names. Unlike the attribute-wrapping damage, this is not fixed by printWidth.
# It is fixable by assembling the attributes in a variable and emitting them with
# safeHTMLAttr - a plain string in attribute-name position trips Hugo's
# contextual escaping and renders ZgotmplZ - but that is a rewrite of each site,
# so they are excluded until then.
layouts/_markup/render-image.html
layouts/_partials/components/analytics/umami.html
layouts/_partials/components/github-style-alert.html
layouts/_partials/shortcodes/article-card.html
layouts/_partials/shortcodes/button.html

# Tab labels sit adjacent with no whitespace between them, deliberately: the
# formatter's indentation becomes real whitespace in .PlainText, turning the meta
# description "JSONYAMLTOML" into "JSON YAML TOML". Arguably nicer, but it is a
# rendered-output change, so this file keeps its current layout.
layouts/_partials/shortcodes/tabs.html

# Prettier reformats its own config into JSONC - it collapses the overrides and
# leaves a trailing comma - which strict JSON.parse consumers then reject.
.prettierrc

# Its doc comment carries `{{< ... >}}` examples, which prettier parses as
# template actions and moves onto their own lines. scripts/build-skill.mjs reads
# each @param and @example to the end of its line, so the wrap truncated the
# generated reference: "prompts=true >}}" became "prompts=true". The doc comments
# are the authority for the shipped skill, so this file keeps its own formatting.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ shortcodes
#
# Every shortcode's doc comment is generated output in reverse: 57 of the 58
# files carry `@param` and `@example` tags, and scripts/build-skill.mjs reads
# them to produce skills/hextra/references/shortcodes.md, the reference shipped
# in the skill package. AGENTS.md calls those comments the authority for
# shortcode parameters.
#
# Prettier cannot be trusted with them. It reads the `{{< ... >}}` examples
# inside a comment as template actions and moves them onto their own lines; a
# `@param` whose description embeds one then loses its closing delimiter, because
# the generator reads each tag to the end of its line. That silently published a
# broken example for `jupyter` and left the generated file stale, which fails
# `build-skill --check` on a clean tree.
#
# Excluding the directory states the real rule - this is where the generator
# reads from - instead of listing files as they break. Formatting these 58 costs
# little: they are hand-written and already consistent. Partials, render hooks
# and layouts are unaffected; the generator does not read them.
layouts/_shortcodes/

